 to control. Diagonals provide better structure. So I select **(C, 3)**.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: There are three valid moves: (C, 3), (C, 5), and (E, 3).\n1.  **(C, 5)**: Places a piece at C5 and flips D5. This creates a vertical line of your pieces (C5, D5, E5) running parallel to the opponent\'s line (C4, D4, E4). Parallel walls are generally weak in the opening because they allow the opponent to easily flank the line (e.g., by playing at D6 or C6), regaining control and leaving you with a split position.\n2.  **(E, 3)**: Places a piece at E3 and flips E4. This creates a horizontal line (E3, E4, E5). Similar to (C, 5), this allows the opponent to flank effectively (e.g., at F4), leaving them with a solid block and you with a potentially vulnerable edge.\n3.  **(C, 3)**: Places a piece at C3 and flips D4. This captures the most "central" piece of the opponent\'s current cluster. By flipping D4, you break up the opponent\'s solid block of pieces (C4, D4, D5, E4). This creates a diagonal connection (C3-D4-E5), which is structurally more robust than a parallel line and makes it harder for the opponent to immediately dominate the board geometry.\n\nReason: Move (C, 3) is the best choice because it breaks the opponent\'s central control by flipping the internal piece D4, rather than just an edge piece. It avoids creating weak parallel wall formations that the opponent can easily exploit, and the resulting diagonal shape offers better long-term mobility.\n\nAnswer: (C, 3)'}
